    {
      "id": "moment-7c744fc5-b9d4-4c1e-8e6a-4f7adfd8b2e7",
      "experienceId": "experience-costa-rica-2025-jurassic-canyon",
      "postId": "7c744fc5-b9d4-4c1e-8e6a-4f7adfd8b2e7",
      "title": "Bajos Del Toro Remainder",
      "notes": "I follow our guide through a power plant and a hole in the fence, then down toward a riverbed. This is the way into Jurassic Canyon in Bajos del Toro. The first stretch is slippery, with cables and vines to hold onto. Getting to the waterfalls is a little more involved than a quick walk.\n\nWe ford ice cold water the color of Arctic ice, jump gaps between slippery rocks, and toss gear across, hoping nobody misses the catch. There are plants with leaves three times my wingspan. But what stays with me is the way the waterfalls reveal themselves as we trek through the canyon. The views are extraordinary, and the way we reach them is part of it.\n\nBajos del Toro was a last minute consideration, and this turns out to be the highlight of the trip. The climb back up takes effort. I'd still give this experience at least six hours, with a guide, rather than rush it. Nothing about our loose plans prepared me for this canyon.",
